    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe overall shape is strong and fits the codebase well: the new `fiscalYear`/`toFiscalYear` methods follow the established `toXxx(Owner)` derivation pattern, and exposing `fiscalYear(LocalDate)` as a `static` \"single source of truth\" that the controller reaches into directly mirrors the existing precedent of `OwnerMapper.toIdentityKey`, `OwnerMapper.regionOf`, and `OwnerMapper.CITY_REGION`. The docs and cross-references are consistent with the surrounding style. The findings below are minor.\n\n1. Inconsistent idiom: fully-qualified type reference (`OwnerMapper.java:67`). `fiscalYear` uses `java.time.Month.JULY.getValue()` inline while every other `java.time` type in this file (`LocalDate`, `Period`) is imported at the top. A maintainer would either add `import java.time.Month;` and write `Month.JULY.getValue()`, or simply use the literal `>= 7`. As written it sticks out against the file's import convention.\n\n2. Thin single-caller wrapper (`OwnerMapper.java:77-79`). `fiscalYearLabel(LocalDate)` is a static method with exactly one caller (`toFiscalYear`). It's defensible as a documented, testable pure function that parallels `fiscalYear`, but it's borderline over-decomposition \u2014 the `String.format(\"FY%02d\", fiscalYear(date) % 100)` body could fold into `toFiscalYear` without loss. Optional; leave as-is if the intent was a separately-testable label formatter.\n\n3. Minor duplicated \"last-two-digits\" formatting. `fiscalYearLabel` does `String.format(\"FY%02d\", fiscalYear(date) % 100)` and the controller's `membershipNumber` (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:437`) does `String.format(\"%02d\", OwnerMapper.fiscalYear(registrationDate) % 100)`. The genuinely shared rule (the fiscal-year computation) is already centralized in `fiscalYear`, and the two call sites differ in prefix (`FY` vs `M`), so this is acceptable and matches what the old code did \u2014 noting only that the `% 100` zero-pad transform now lives 